Cascadia: a game streamed over HTTP ran the iPad 2 out of memory (God of
War: Ghost of Sparta, OOM-killed minutes into play).  PPSSPP keeps up to
256 MB of a streamed disc in RAM (CachingFileLoader: 4096 blocks of 64 KB)
and drops nothing before that, while the disk cache under it turns itself
off where there is no room for it: with ~470 MB of RAM and no swap, every
MB streamed stayed.  Now:
- the blocks kept: a sixteenth of the RAM at most (~29 MB there; 256 MB as
  before from 4 GB on), or PPSSPP_DISC_CACHE_MB's;
- a read-ahead makes its room as a read does, the least recently used
  blocks going (upstream: no read-ahead into a full cache -- with a small
  one, full all the time, read-ahead would stop for good), and the blocks
  it reads count as used just now (upstream: as never used, the first to
  go, before they are read);
- the blocks are counted as they go in (upstream added all of a read's,
  those another thread had read meanwhile too: the count crept past the
  blocks there were, and an emptied cache's eviction loop never ended).

